# French liaison and final consonant deletion: the worked example of
# DELETION, and of a rule conditioned across a word boundary.
#
# With this file each of the four things the notation can do has a shipped
# example: american-english.rules is all substitution and feature change,
# the two loanword sets are insertion, and this one is deletion.
#
# The phenomenon. A French word-final consonant is LATENT: silent before
# a consonant or a pause, pronounced before a vowel, where it attaches to
# the following word as an onset.
#
#     lez‿ami     les amis      -> [le‿zami]     the /z/ surfaces
#     pətit‿ami   petit ami     -> [pəti‿tami]   the /t/ surfaces
#     lez‿ʃjɛ̃     les chiens    -> [le‿ʃjɛ̃]      the /z/ stays silent
#     pətit‿ʃjɛ̃   petit chien   -> [pəti‿ʃjɛ̃]    the /t/ stays silent
#     lez         les (alone)   -> [le]          nothing follows
#
# DELETING, NOT INSERTING, and the direction is a choice worth arguing.
# The other direction -- lexical /le/, with the /z/ inserted before a
# vowel -- would produce the same four forms above and is wrong for two
# reasons that show up as soon as the corpus grows past four phrases.
# First, WHICH consonant appears is unpredictable from the phonology:
# 'les' links with /z/, 'petit' with /t/, 'mon' with /n/, 'trop' with /p/.
# An inserting account has to look the consonant up per word, which is a
# lexicon, not a rule; a deleting account reads
# it off the lexical form, which is where it already is. Second, the
# consonant is audible in the word's other shapes -- 'petit'/'petite',
# 'bon'/'bonne' -- so it is in the lexical entry whether liaison likes it
# or not. Deletion is the classical treatment (Schane, Dell, Tranel) for
# exactly these reasons.
#
# THE INPUT. A phonological phrase, with:
#   * latent consonants PRESENT -- 'lez', 'pətit', 'mɔ̃n';
#   * word division written with '‿', the declared IPA linking mark, where
#     the words are in one phonological phrase, and with a space or '#'
#     where there is a break;
#   * a final schwa written where the language has one -- 'pətitə' for
#     'petite', 'bɔnə' for 'bonne'. That schwa is what protects the
#     consonant in front of it, and the ordering below is what makes it
#     protect it.
#
# '‿' is the right mark and not a convenience: docs/ties.md defines it as
# absence of a break between words, and names French liaison as its use.
# Using it rather than a space is also a substantive claim -- liaison
# happens inside a phonological phrase and not across a pause -- so
# 'lez ami' with a space correctly gives [le ami], with the /z/ lost.
#
# '‿' declares level="word" and linking="+": it DIVIDES two words while
# saying they are run together, so it sits on the word tier and '#'
# reaches it. That is what makes the two halves of this set ask their
# questions differently, and the difference is the analysis:
#
#   * DELETION asks "is this the end of a word", so it is written '_ #',
#     which reaches the linking mark, a space, and the form's own edge
#     alike -- the three ways this set's input spells a word end.
#   * LIAISON asks the narrower question "is this the end of a word inside
#     one phonological phrase", so it NAMES '‿'. MEASURED, written with
#     '#' the copy rule fires across a space too: '∅ -> z / z # _ [vowel]'
#     takes 'lez ami' to 'lez zami', licensing liaison over the pause the
#     space asserts. Naming the mark is how the rule states its domain.

# --- liaison ------------------------------------------------------------
# The latent consonant surfaces as the ONSET of the following word, not as
# the coda of its own: 'les amis' is [le.za.mi], syllabified across the
# join. That is resyllabification, which is movement, and movement --
# metathesis -- is deliberately not expressible here (docs/rules.md). It
# is expressible as a copy followed by a deletion, and since the deletion
# is a rule this set needs anyway, the copy is the only rule the analysis
# adds. Ordering does the rest.
#
# One line per latent consonant, and the reason is a variable over a
# SEGMENT, which is a different thing from the agreement variable the
# notation now has. What the rule wants to say is "insert a copy of the
# consonant to the left of the boundary": not "the same value of one
# feature here and there", which is what 'α' says, but the whole segment.
# So the segment has to be NAMED, and it appears on both sides of the
# same line, four times over.
#
# THIS NOTE USED TO SAY american-english.rules HIT THE SAME WALL, and it
# no longer does: nasal place assimilation there is now one rule with
# 'α' in it. The wall these four lines are against is the one docs/
# calculus.md still lists -- a variable over a segment, not over a value.
#
# The context names '‿' for the domain reason above, and it would have to
# name some boundary in any case: MEASURED, the linking mark is OPAQUE to
# context scanning -- 'word' is opaque, and opacity is read off the level
# -- so a rule that does not name it cannot see past it.
# 't -> ɾ / [vowel] _ [vowel]' fires on 'ata' and not on 'a‿ta'. Here that
# opacity is useful. Elsewhere it is a trap, and the note at the end of
# this file says why.
∅ -> z / z ‿ _ [vowel] ; liaison (z)
∅ -> t / t ‿ _ [vowel] ; liaison (t)
∅ -> n / n ‿ _ [vowel] ; liaison (n)
∅ -> p / p ‿ _ [vowel] ; liaison (p)

# --- /ʁ/ IS NOT ON THAT LIST, and it used to be --------------------------
# This is the one place where the set's central simplification -- that a
# final /z t n p/ is treated as latent because nothing in the segment says
# whether it is -- was applied to a segment where it is false far more
# often than it is true, and the file's own reasoning for including it is
# what was wrong.
#
# The reasoning was 'premier étage' [pʁəmje‿ʁetaʒ]: latent /ʁ/ is real, so
# the segment joined the list. But latent /ʁ/ is a closed and largely
# obsolete class -- the -er infinitives and 'premier'/'dernier', where the
# liaison is bookish and most speakers do not make it -- while STABLE
# final /ʁ/ is the majority case of the language: every -eur, -oir and -ir
# noun, every -ir infinitive, and the commonest words in it. MEASURED,
# with the two /ʁ/ rules in place: 'bɔ̃ʒuʁ' -> [bɔ̃ʒu], 'mɛʁ' -> [mɛ],
# 'puʁ' -> [pu], 'syʁ' -> [sy], 'kaʁ' -> [ka]. Five words, five wrong
# answers, against one archaic phrase repaired.
#
# For /z t n p/ the same simplification is benign, and the file already
# says why a page up: the stable-final exceptions there are a short list
# ('sept', 'net', 'avec'-like), and the protecting schwa handles the
# regular alternations. The arithmetic is what differs, not the analysis.
# So the rule is not repaired, it is REMOVED: a latent /ʁ/ needs the
# lexical mark that this set does not have, and until it does, deleting
# nothing is much closer to French than deleting every final /ʁ/.
#
# 'pʁəmjeʁ‿etaʒ' therefore comes out unchanged, and that is the residue
# being paid for. It is in the corpus as a pin, so the cost stays visible.

# --- final consonant deletion -------------------------------------------
# ORDERING (1 of 2). These rules ask nothing about what follows the
# boundary, and they can afford not to BECAUSE the liaison rules have
# already run: where liaison applied there is now a second copy of the
# consonant safely inside the next word, so deleting the original is
# exactly resyllabification. Run the
# other way round, 'lez‿ami' comes out [le‿ami] -- the /z/ deleted before
# the rule that would have saved it ever looked. That is bleeding, and it
# is measured rather than asserted.
#
# ONE CONTEXT PER CONSONANT, and it was two until the linking mark
# declared a level. What the rule needs is "at the end of a word": before
# a linked word ('lez‿ʃjɛ̃'), before a break ('lez ʃjɛ̃'), or at the end of
# the form with nothing typed ('lez'), which is the pause case. '_ #' is
# that set, because the tiers nest and all three are word-level.
#
# It was not always '#', and the history is worth keeping because the
# wrong spelling is still available. While '‿' declared no level it sat on
# no tier, so '#' did not reach it and the only pattern that did was '%'.
# '%' is "any boundary", so it also reaches the syllable dot, and a dot is
# word-INTERNAL. Do NOT "simplify" '_ #' to '_ %'. RE-MEASURED, on this
# set as it stands: with '_ %' throughout, 28 of the 143 dotted spellings
# of this set's own corpus come out differently from their undotted twins,
# against 0 with '_ #'. (It was 30 of 106 before this file lost its two
# /ʁ/ rules and gained the clitics, and 24 of 122 before the corpus gained
# the four e caduc words; the corpus moves, so the count moves, and the
# ratio is the claim rather than the integer.) Any dot after a
# deletable consonant is enough --
# 'z -> ∅ / _ %' takes 'lez‿z.ami' to [le‿.ami], deleting the liaison
# consonant this set had just placed, where '_ #' gives [le‿z.ami] and
# leaves it alone. That is the exact failure the transparency rule exists
# to prevent -- one word, two answers, decided by who typed the dots.
#
# Naming the two boundaries separately -- '_ #' and '_ ‿' on consecutive
# lines -- was the workaround while the level was missing, and it is worth
# saying what removing it changed, because "shadowed" is the wrong
# diagnosis.
#
# THE SWEEP BEHIND THE NEXT TWO CLAIMS IS DEFINED HERE, because the
# numbers that stood in this paragraph were measured over "30927
# re-spelling forms" and nothing recorded how those forms were built, so
# nobody could re-measure them -- which is how a MEASURED claim rots. The
# sweep is now: every form of one to three segments drawn from
# {z t n p ə a}, each segment optionally followed by one of {. ‿ # space},
# keeping the forms that spell themselves back. That is 27930 forms, and
# it is written down so the next reader can run it.
#
# Over it, the positions '_ ‿' holds at are a strict SUBSET of the
# positions '_ #' holds at, for every one of these five segments. So the
# pair states what '_ #' states alone: it is REDUNDANT, and the second
# line of each pair never firing is a consequence of the wider rule
# standing first, not an ordering to repair. Putting the narrow line first
# does make both fire -- and says the same thing in twice the lines.
#
# It was not inert in general, though: running the same deletion twice let
# it ITERATE, so a final geminate lost both members. Over that sweep the
# one-line and two-line spellings differ exactly where a form ends in two
# identical deletable segments -- adjacent or separated only by a dot --
# at the point the rule looks, and every difference is one or two extra
# segments deleted. Most such forms carry the doubled pair in the input;
# the rest acquire it when an earlier deletion takes away what stood
# between the pair and the boundary. 'zz‿' and 'z.z‿' came out [‿]
# and [.‿] where one rule per consonant gives [z‿] and [z.‿]. One deletion
# per word end is what the analysis says, so the collapse fixed that too.
z -> ∅ / _ # ; final z deletion
t -> ∅ / _ # ; final t deletion
n -> ∅ / _ # ; final n deletion
p -> ∅ / _ # ; final p deletion

# Latency is LEXICAL, and this is where that bites. These rules delete a
# final /z t n p/ from any word that has one, so 'sept' [sɛt] and 'net'
# [nɛt] and 'avec'-like exceptions come out wrong; the rules cannot tell a
# latent consonant from a stable one, because nothing in the segment says
# which it is. What does the work for the regular cases is the protecting
# schwa and the ordering below -- 'petite' /pətitə/ keeps its /t/ -- and
# the residue is a list of exceptions, which is a lexicon and not
# something a rule set should pretend to derive.

# --- final schwa deletion -----------------------------------------------
# ORDERING (2 of 2), and the classical demonstration that rule order is
# part of the grammar. 'petite' is lexically /pətitə/ and 'petit'
# /pətit/; both surface with the schwa gone, but only one loses its /t/.
# Ordered LAST, the schwa is still in place when final consonant deletion
# looks, so it shelters the /t/, and the two words come out [pətit] and
# [pəti]. Ordered FIRST, /pətitə/ becomes /pətit/ before deletion runs
# and both come out [pəti] -- the contrast is destroyed. Measured.
#
# This is the same relation as the liaison rules above, seen from the
# other side: there ordering SAVES a consonant by putting a copy out of
# reach, here it saves one by leaving a shield in place.
#
# '#' reaches the linking mark, so 'pətitə‿ami' loses its schwa without a
# second rule naming '‿'.
#
# TWO LINES, because a word may not be left without a vowel. Written as
# the bare 'ə -> ∅ / _ #' this rule stripped the clitics to a consonant:
# MEASURED, 'lə' -> [l], 'ʒə' -> [ʒ], 'sə' -> [s], which are not French
# words but French CONSONANTS. The schwa of 'le', 'je', 'ce', 'de', 'ne',
# 'que' is the only vowel those words have, and it is retained for exactly
# that reason -- 'le chien' is [lə ʃjɛ̃] -- while 'une' [yn] and 'bonne'
# [bɔn] drop theirs and keep a vowel each.
#
# The condition wanted is "the word has another vowel", which is a claim
# about a CONSTITUENT and not about the neighbors -- the same divergence
# american-english.rules keeps running into. What is available is the
# neighbors, and here they are enough, because the failing case is
# exactly one shape: a schwa behind a single word-initial consonant. So
# the rule asks for one more unit to the left of that consonant, whatever
# it is, and since a bracketed query is a conjunction there is no way to
# say "anything" once -- it is a vowel, or it is not. Two lines, and the
# pair is the union that 'not # C _ #' would have been.
#
# 'katʁə' -> [katʁ] falls out of the second line, which is right, and
# leaves this set standing where the note at the foot of the file already
# puts it: whether that /ʁ/ then drops is the three-consonant constraint,
# which is not modeled here.
ə -> ∅ / [vowel] [-vowel] _ # ; final schwa deletion
ə -> ∅ / [-vowel] [-vowel] _ # ; final schwa deletion (after a cluster)

# --- e caduc: the OPTIONAL half, and why it needs a different arrow ------
# Everything above is obligatory: one form in, one form out. Word-internal
# schwa is not like that. 'petit' is [pəti] AND [pti], from the same
# speaker in the same conversation, and neither is the derived form of the
# other -- they are two pronunciations of one word. A grammar that has to
# pick one is not describing French; it is picking one.
#
# '~>' is that arrow (docs/calculus.md). The rule MAY fire, so a cascade
# carrying one derives a SET:
#
#     ipakit rules variants -s french-liaison pətit    -> pətit, ptit
#
# 'rules apply' still answers with one form and takes no optional choice,
# so everything above this line derives exactly as it did before.
#
# THE CONDITION IS THE LOI DES TROIS CONSONNES, and stating it is the
# whole difficulty. A schwa may drop where dropping it does not leave
# three consonants in a row: 'samedi' [samdi] but 'vendredi' *[vɑ̃dʁdi].
# What the notation offers is neighbors, and here they are enough said
# in the negative: at most ONE consonant on the left of the schwa, and
# that consonant preceded by a vowel or by a word edge; at most one on
# the right, and that one followed by a vowel. So 'vɑ̃dʁədi' matches
# neither line -- the /ʁ/ before the schwa is itself behind a /d/ -- and
# comes out with its schwa, which is right.
#
# TWO LINES, and the split is NOT cosmetic. It is the one place where the
# engine's own model shows through, so it is worth spelling out.
#
# Within a single rule every site is found against a SNAPSHOT, and under
# '~>' each site then branches INDEPENDENTLY. That is what the phenomenon
# wants -- a word with two schwas has each independently droppable -- and
# it is also how a constraint on the RESULT gets lost, because no site can
# see what another site chose. 'devenir' /dəvəniʁ/ is the classical
# demonstration: [dəvəniʁ], [dvəniʁ] and [dəvniʁ] are all French and
# *[dvniʁ] is not, and one rule matching both schwas at once derives all
# four. MEASURED: written as the single line
# 'ə ~> ∅ / [-vowel] _ [-vowel] [vowel]' the set is
# {dəvəniʁ, dvəniʁ, dəvniʁ, dvniʁ}.
#
# Ordered rules are where the missing information is. Each rule sees the
# previous rule's OUTPUT, branch by branch, so the second line asks its
# question of a form in which the first line's choice has already been
# made: in the branch where the first schwa went, /v/ now stands behind
# /d/ and the interior line does not match. MEASURED, as the two lines
# below: {dəvəniʁ, dəvniʁ, dvəniʁ} -- the three attested, and the fourth
# excluded by the grammar rather than by a filter afterwards.
#
# So: a constraint over the result of several optional choices is stated
# by SPLITTING the choices over ordered rules. That is the same tool
# ordering has always been in this file, applied to a new question, and
# it is written up as a technique a grammar-writer can reach for in
# docs/calculus.md, under "Splitting the choices over ordered rules" --
# what the pattern is, the three conditions it needs, and the word it
# gets wrong. It is recorded there as a property of the calculus, not of
# French.
#
# AND IT IS NARROWER THAN THE CONSTRAINT IT STANDS IN FOR. Read that
# section before relying on these two lines. The technique separates
# choices it can put in DIFFERENT rules; two sites that fall to the SAME
# rule still branch independently against the snapshot, because that is
# what the limit says and no ordering reaches inside one rule.
#
# MEASURED, on a real word one derivation longer than 'devenir':
# 'redevenir' /ʁədəvəniʁ/ has three droppable schwas, and the second and
# third are BOTH interior -- one rule's two sites. The set is
# {ʁədəvəniʁ, ʁədvəniʁ, ʁədəvniʁ, ʁədvniʁ, ʁdəvəniʁ, ʁdəvniʁ}, and the
# fourth is *[ʁədvniʁ]: /d v n/, which the loi des trois consonnes
# forbids exactly as it forbids *[dvniʁ]. One of six here where one rule
# gave one of four there. The split improved the ratio and did not change
# the kind, and this file over-generates on 'redevenir' today.
#
# THE SPLIT IS NOT A PREFERENCE for the first schwa over the second, and
# that was measured rather than assumed: reversing the two lines gives
# the same three forms for 'devenir' in a different derivational order,
# because the bleeding runs both ways -- after the interior schwa drops,
# 'dəvniʁ' no longer offers the first line its '_ [-vowel] [vowel]'.
ə ~> ∅ / # [-vowel] _ [-vowel] [vowel] ; e caduc (first syllable)
ə ~> ∅ / [vowel] [-vowel] _ [-vowel] [vowel] ; e caduc (interior)

# --- what this file found out about '‿', recorded rather than fixed -----
# Three behaviors of the linking mark are load-bearing here and none is
# obvious. All were measured; none is changed by this file, since '‿' is
# declared in ipakit/data/ipa.xml and read by ipakit/form.py, which this
# set does not own.
#
# 1. '‿' IS OPAQUE to context scanning, and that now follows from the
#    declaration rather than from the absence of one: opacity is read off
#    the level, and 'word' is opaque. A syllable dot is transparent
#    precisely so that optional notation cannot change which rules fire;
#    the linking mark, whose declared meaning is *absence* of a break, is
#    not. So marking a phrase's liaison changes what every other rule can
#    see: 't -> ɾ / [vowel] _ [vowel]' fires on 'ata' and not on 'a‿ta'.
#    This set benefits, because naming the mark is how liaison states its
#    domain -- but a French set that also wanted, say, intervocalic
#    voicing would find it silently blocked at exactly the join liaison
#    just created. In 'le‿zami' the /z/ cannot see the /e/ that licensed
#    it. Whether a mark meaning "no pause" ought to be transparent is a
#    question about the declaration, not about this file.
#
# 2. '#' NOW REACHES '‿', which is why every deletion above is one line.
#    It did not while the mark declared no 'level': nothing in the level
#    ladder reached a division between words that claimed no tier, '%' was
#    the only pattern that did, and '%' also reaches the syllable dot. The
#    two were on opposite sides of what a word-final rule wants with
#    nothing between them. Declaring level="word" put the mark on the
#    ladder and closed the gap; '%' is still too wide and still wrong here.
#
# 3. A DOT IMMEDIATELY AFTER THE MARK used to cost this set its liaison.
#    FIXED, and this note is kept because the failure shape is subtle.
#    'lez‿.ami' now gives [le‿.zami] -- the copy lands -- and 0 of the 143
#    dotted spellings of this set's corpus differ from their undotted
#    twins. It was never about these lines: '‿.' is a boundary RUN, a run
#    offers one gap, and the gap after the run counted as an anchor only
#    when the left context matched the run's LAST mark -- here it matched
#    '‿', which the dot had displaced from last position, so the copy had
#    nowhere to land. _anchors now licenses the trailing gap on ANY mark
#    the context matched. Closed by rules.py at 2e81eec and pinned by
#    test_a_dot_after_the_linking_mark_keeps_the_liaison_it_licensed.
#    DO NOT "repair" _anchors to match an older reading of this note: that
#    function has been fixed twice and reintroducing the last-mark test is
#    exactly the regression the pin exists to catch.
#
# A rule CAN insert '‿' -- '∅ -> ‿ / [vowel] _ z [vowel]' turns 'lezami'
# into 'le‿zami' -- so a set that took unmarked input could mark the
# liaison it licenses. This set does not, and the reason has changed
# since it was written. Inserting the mark beside a space would ADD it
# rather than replace it ('lez ‿ami', two boundary units); what was then
# refused, rewriting one boundary as another, is now expressible --
# '# -> ‿' puts the mark in the space's place, since a boundary may be
# written, unwritten or restated at another level (ipakit/rules.py).
# What a .rules FILE still cannot do is target '#', because a line
# beginning with '#' is a comment -- as every line of this one is. So
# taking '‿' in the input remains the honest way round it here.

# --- deliberately not modeled ------------------------------------------
# * Optional and forbidden liaison. French liaison is obligatory in some
#   contexts ('les amis'), optional in others ('c'est étrange'), and
#   forbidden in a third set ('et', 'h aspiré', after a singular noun).
#   The distinction is syntactic and lexical, and the input's use of '‿'
#   is where a caller states it: mark the join and liaison applies, write
#   a space and it does not. That is a way of ASKING the question, not an
#   answer to it.
#
#   '~>' does not change that, and it is worth saying why, because it
#   looks as though it should. Marking the four liaison rules optional
#   would derive [le‿ami] beside [le‿zami] -- and [le‿ami] is not French,
#   it is a speech error. The arrow says "this may or may not happen",
#   which is true of e caduc and false of liaison in 'les amis'; WHICH
#   liaisons are the optional ones is the syntactic fact the set does not
#   have. An optional arrow on a rule whose optionality is conditioned by
#   something the rule cannot see is over-generation with a new spelling.
# * A weighting over the variants. The set 'variants' returns is UNRANKED:
#   [pti] is commoner than [pəti] in most registers and the calculus has
#   no way to say so. Frequency is a fact about speakers, not about the
#   grammar, and a rule set is not where it would go.
# * Liaison consonants that differ from the lexical one: 'grand' links
#   with [t] though the word ends in /d/, 'neuf ans' with [v]. A rule per
#   pair could state these; they are left out because each is a lexical
#   fact rather than a generalization.
# * Enchaînement, which resyllabifies a STABLE final consonant across a
#   join ('avec elle' [a.vɛ.kɛl]) with no deletion anywhere. It is the
#   same resyllabification without the latency, so the copy rules here
#   would state it and the deletion rules must not reach it -- which
#   needs the lexical distinction this set does not have.
# * Nasal vowel denasalization before a liaison /n/ ('bon ami' is
#   [bɔ.na.mi] for many speakers, [bɔ̃.na.mi] for others).
# * The schwa's other lives: 'e caduc' inside a word, and the three-
#   consonant constraint that decides whether it drops.
